WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.980
there's a new study from Yale university,
the university of Florida and the university

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:12.169
of Maryland. And what this study says is that
transitioning to a single payer healthcare

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:18.970
system here in the United States would save
about $450 billion per year. It would save

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:28.420
the average family $2,400 a year and it would
prevent 68,000 unnecessary deaths representing

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:37.110
in total about a 13% savings in annual healthcare
costs with increased access. Now, you all

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:43.630
know that I've talked a lot about Medicare
for all and sometimes my commentaries about

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:50.530
Medicare for all, um, anger, some people in
the audience and typically those commentaries

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.329
are ones where I make clear that I support
Medicare for all. And I believe it may well

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:01.850
be the best system, but I believe that the
practical difficulties of making Medicare

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.500
for all a reality are often I'm a realist.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:11.220
That's true. And a lot of people hear my realism
and they call me a defeatist. Now this is

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:17.090
a realist show. This is not a show that gives
you fantasies. So we discuss the difficulties

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:22.140
of policy and you might have noticed, maybe
you didn't that Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.740
Cortez recently similarly said, you know,
even if Bernie is elected, you don't just

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.530
wave a magic wand and get Medicare for all.
She wasn't attacked for saying basically the

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:38.520
same thing as I did. But that's another story.
Let's take some time today to in a non hyper-partisan

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.369
way think through this very good study, which
shows that Medicare for all would be cheaper

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:49.330
and it would prevent a lot of unnecessary
deaths. And let's apply it to our thinking

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:53.560
about healthcare altogether. There's a great
healthcare scholar who you've maybe heard

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.790
me mention before he passed away not long
ago. His name is [inaudible] Reinhardt.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:05.439
Okay. And in his writing, [inaudible] would
explain that. When you look at healthcare,

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:11.930
there are essentially three factors to consider,
cost, access, and quality. Okay? If you pick

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:18.230
a certain healthcare cost, imagine you say
$100 billion is our healthcare costs. You

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:24.060
can then choose a balance between how much
access is there for 100 billion and how much

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:30.120
quality is there for 100 billion so to, to
make it visual. Imagine you have a slider.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.860
It's that we have $100 billion to spend on
healthcare and you have a slider and you can

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.950
either give that hundred billion in healthcare
spending to one person, right? If you give

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.730
the a hundred billion to one person, you have
very low access. Only one person has healthcare,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.340
but they're going to get everything they need
for that. $100 billion quality is going to

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:55.629
be absolutely huge. Or you go to the other
side and you say, well, let's give 5 billion

00:02:55.629 --> 00:03:00.260
people coverage for that hundred a a billion
dollars in healthcare spending, and you can

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:06.530
imagine how now access has gone up to a hundred
percent but quality is going to go down and

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:07.530
you're not.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.220
If you split the 100 billion among 5 billion
people, instead of giving it all to one person,

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.640
the quality of care is going to go down. Now,
that's an extreme example to get us thinking

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:22.640
in this sort of three metric mode, everybody
has healthcare, so now imagine a different

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:29.950
slider. We have a different slider called
100% access, okay? In this scenario, everybody

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:37.440
has healthcare, but now you've got a balance,
cost and quality, right? 100% access is fixed.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.650
All else being equal. If you want quality
to be high with 100% access, you're going

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:50.760
to have higher costs. If you want costs to
be low under 100% access, then you are going

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.930
to have lower quality, right? If 5 billion
people get crappy care, you keep costs low

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:04.670
to give 5 billion people better care, costs
going to go up. So I think you get the idea,

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.990
these are the three metrics, cost, access
and quality and any health care program, I,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.629
I, this isn't even yet about Medicare for
all.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:21.199
Any system we have is going to somehow balance
cost, access and quality. If you don't change

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:27.180
the system, but you just give everybody access,
the only way to get it to cost less is to

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:32.900
reduce quality of care unless you fix other
things. And this is what's at the crux of

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.669
the Medicare for all debate. We say, Hey,
you know what? We can increase access and

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:45.240
we can keep quality the same or better. Well,
only if you deal with cost. If you don't deal

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.150
with the cost of the system, costs will go
up to keep the same quality and to give more

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:55.150
people healthcare. And so that's where you
get to a lot of the ways in which a program

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.639
like Medicare for all can actually reduce
costs without affecting quality necessarily.

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:04.469
So you can remove a lot of the middlemen that
inflate costs. You can take away the profit

00:05:04.469 --> 00:05:08.259
part for health insurance companies, which
is actually relatively low.

00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:13.800
You can eliminate all these multiple layers
of medical billing and bureaucracy and unnecessary

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.860
medical testing. You know, you can do all
that stuff. Um, but that's more of a gray

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.849
area. And the biggest criticism of most Medicare
for all plans is not, you know, from this

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:29.249
ideological it's socialism perspective that
those are dumb critiques. The serious critiques

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:36.969
of Medicare for all are that most of the plans
way too generously assume massive cost cuts

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:42.899
that they claim won't affect quality in order
to get everybody access without costing more

00:05:42.899 --> 00:05:48.759
and without sacrificing quality. That gets
us to this new study. This new study says

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:56.319
we could transition to Medicare for all. We
will save $450 billion per year. We will save

00:05:56.319 --> 00:06:02.259
68,000 people from unnecessary death and everybody
will get access. So when we go to our three

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:08.089
metrics, they are saying cost will go down
and access will go up.

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:14.400
They don't say anything about quality. So
the critique of this study is that it is not

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.830
realistic in terms of the quality of care
that will be provided. This is not my criticism.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:27.400
This is the reality cost, access quality.
Unless you can explain here's how we reduce

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:34.160
costs in order to increase access without
reducing quality. This is how it works. These

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.210
are the three factors, factors and studies
that say you can get massive savings just

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.030
from making some small tweaks. You're often
only talking about a couple of percentage

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.710
points and case in point, this study has been
analyzed by the economic policy Institute

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.999
and the economic policy Institute said the
cost savings that are predicted are on the

00:06:52.999 --> 00:06:59.300
high side. This is not a surprise to us. So
again, am I against a system like Medicare

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.399
for all? No, I'm for it. I'm for it.

00:07:01.399 --> 00:07:06.050
I'm for it. I'm for Medicare for all. I'm
in favor of Medicare for all. What I'm telling

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:12.199
you is if you want to increase access to 100%
which I do, if you want to keep quality the

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:19.490
same or increase it, which I do, it most likely
is going to cost much more than what any candidate

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:26.270
is currently claiming. And that's okay. We
can say as a society it will cost more in

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:32.949
total, but you'll save premiums and copays.
We'll separate insurance from employers so

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:40.179
people don't have to tie their insurance to
a job. We'll reduce the cost per person. Rich

00:07:40.179 --> 00:07:45.469
people will have to pay quite a bit more in
order to subsidize the system. In total, it

00:07:45.469 --> 00:07:51.259
will cost more. I'm okay with that. I'm okay
because I think we need the same or better

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:55.610
quality than what we have now and we need
100% access.

00:07:55.610 --> 00:08:01.679
I'm okay paying more. Uh, if that's what it
takes, but we need to understand the reality

00:08:01.679 --> 00:08:08.559
of the plans and a lot of the plans are assuming
if we switch to Medicare for all American

00:08:08.559 --> 00:08:14.289
healthcare costs, per capita will drop from
what they are here to what Australia or Canada

00:08:14.289 --> 00:08:18.550
pay. That's not going to happen by making
the small tweaks that a lot of people are

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:23.490
talking about, it's going to require much,
much more. Yes, we've got to eliminate waste,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:29.399
but to match the per capita costs of Australia
and Canada, eliminating wet waste just isn't

00:08:29.399 --> 00:08:35.180
enough and we've got to figure that out and
we need to have answers because I want a system

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:41.389
like this. I hope that my analysis is making
sense. I want to hear from you. Remember I'm

00:08:41.389 --> 00:08:45.779
for Medicare for all. If you're about to email
me and tell me how terrible it is that I'm

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:47.730
again a followed the David package.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:51.380
Michelle show on Instagram at David Pakman
show. While you're there, follow me on Instagram

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:51.389
at David's

